    67	### Hobby front proxy
    68	
    69	Vercel Hobby does not provide edge rate-limiting rules. This repository includes a small Cloudflare Worker at `cloudflare/front-proxy.ts` that can run on the free `workers.dev` hostname instead. It only accepts the fetch endpoint, applies a Cloudflare Worker Rate Limiting binding (30 requests/minute per IP per Cloudflare location), forwards to the fixed Vercel origin, and strips all inbound headers except the browser signal needed for admission. Cloudflare Workers Free currently allows 100,000 Worker requests/day; the Vercel function's global Upstash limit remains the second layer.
    70	
    71	Deploy it with `npm run deploy:worker` after setting the Worker secret `FETCH_FRONT_SHARED_SECRET`. Set the same secret as a sensitive Vercel Production and Preview variable. Then set `VITE_FETCH_PROXY_ORIGIN` to the resulting `https://<worker>.<account-subdomain>.workers.dev` URL and redeploy the plugin. The secret is never included in browser code. When the secret is configured, direct requests to the Vercel function are rejected; only the Worker can reach it.
    72	
    73	The Worker URL is intentionally used instead of a customer-owned custom domain. A Route 53 record by itself would not provide Cloudflare proxying, and `capecod.world` remains on Vercel DNS for its existing project.
    74	
    75	### Egress identity
    76	
    77	Third parties can identify and contact this service from access logs alone:
    78	
    79	- **User-Agent** — every outbound fetch sends the fixed string `ultimate-html-to-penpot/0.2 (+https://github.com/petercr/ultimate-html-penpot-ext)`, which resolves to this repository.
    80	- **security.txt** — published at `/.well-known/security.txt` (RFC 9116) with the abuse/report contact (GitHub Issues) and an `Expires` date that CI enforces is renewed at least 30 days ahead.
    81	
    82	**Static egress IPs: deferred (documented decision).** Vercel's Static IPs are a paid plan add-on, unavailable on our Hobby deployment, and current volume does not justify a dedicated proxy VM. Trade-offs accepted for now: fetches originate from ephemeral Vercel egress; Vercel publishes its egress IP ranges publicly, so site owners can allow- or block-list them wholesale; the fixed User-Agent and `security.txt` provide identification and contact regardless. Revisit before listing on Penpot Hub or if blocks or abuse complaints appear.
    83	
    84	**robots.txt policy: not honored.** The service fetches single pages at the explicit direction of a Penpot user — user-agent semantics, like a browser — rather than crawling: it never follows links, fetches at most one page per user action, and throttles per target origin (30/minute globally via Upstash). `robots.txt` governs autonomous crawlers, not user-initiated fetches. Site owners who prefer to exclude this service can block the fixed User-Agent above at their firewall, or rate-limit it, and can reach us through `security.txt`.
    85	
    86	Every request through the service is disclosed in the plugin UI error text ("the import service"), and the plugin always attempts a credential-free direct browser fetch first, using the service only when the direct request is blocked by CORS or network failure.
    87	
    88	Hosting costs scale with usage; the rate limits above cap worst-case bandwidth per instance. Review Vercel function quotas before enabling the service publicly.
    89	
    90	## Production deployment
    91	
    92	The v0.1.0 release is designed for static hosting on Vercel. Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, or any HTTPS static host that supports response headers also works.
    93	
    94	### Vercel
    95	
    96	1. Import this repository into Vercel (Other framework preset).
    97	2. Use `npm ci && npm run build` as the build command.
    98	3. Set the output directory to `dist`.
    99	4. To enable the URL import service, set `VITE_FETCH_PROXY_ORIGIN` to the deployed Worker URL so plugin builds use the rate-limited front proxy, and keep `FETCH_SERVICE_DISABLED` unset.
   100	5. Keep the generated production hostname stable; the install URL is `https://<hostname>/manifest.json`.
   101	6. Install that manifest URL in a fresh Penpot account before submitting it to Penpot Hub.
   102	
   103	The committed `vercel.json` sends CORS and security headers for every static path while excluding `/api`, where the fetch service manages its own headers, and adds an edge `challenge` for `/api/fetch-html` when `Sec-Fetch-Site` is missing (Hobby-compatible; blocks naive scripts before function cost). Optionally set `UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL`/`UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN` (Upstash free tier) for global rate limiting across instances. Optionally set `SAFE_BROWSING_API_KEY` (Google Safe Browsing Lookup API, free tier) to enable target reputation screening. The included Cloudflare Worker provides the free edge rate limit without requiring Vercel Pro WAF. Static caching applies to `manifest.json`, `plugin.js`, and hashed UI assets.
   104	
   105	### Cloudflare Pages or Netlify
   106	
   107	Use the same build command (`npm ci && npm run build`) and publish the `dist` directory. The committed `public/_headers` file carries the identical CORS and cache policy for those hosts. The URL import service requires a runtime host (Vercel functions today); without one, builds simply omit it and URL imports rely on browser CORS alone.
   108	
   109	Pull requests and pushes to `main` run tests, the API typecheck (`npm run check:api`), and the production build in GitHub Actions, including a bundle check that validates the manifest paths, permissions, icon, CORS headers, and the Vercel configuration.
   110	
   111	## Privacy and security
   112	
   113	- Pasted HTML is parsed and rendered inside the plugin's browser sandbox. It is not uploaded to a service operated by this project.
   114	- Remote assets are requested directly from the asset's original host without credentials. Those hosts receive ordinary network request metadata such as the user's IP address.
   115	- Direct page URL imports also use credential-free browser requests. When they are blocked by CORS **and** the hosted fetch service is configured, the requested page or explicitly requested asset URL is sent to this project's service so it can fetch that resource server-side within the mode-specific limits described above and return the bytes to the plugin. It does not receive user identity beyond IP-derived rate limiting (stored only as a hash), does not retain content, and never forwards credentials. Pasted HTML is never sent through the service.
   116	- Remote SVG inlining, webfont inlining, and trusted-mode same-origin page requests use the same constrained service path (`mode=svg`, `mode=font`/`mode=css`, and `mode=asset`) when direct fetching fails or browser CORS would block the capture.
   117	- Running scripts is off by default and should be enabled only for trusted source HTML.
   118	- The plugin requests Penpot's `content:write` permission so it can create editable boards and layers.
   119	
   120	Threat model summary for the service: it is an abuse-resistant partial web proxy for public pages only. SSRF defenses (address classification, pinned connections, redirect revalidation) protect internal networks; size/time/rate caps limit resource abuse; fixed outbound headers and no-forward rules prevent credential propagation; structured logs contain no page content or raw client IPs and are subject to the hosting platform's log retention. Do not paste confidential HTML or enable scripts from an untrusted source.
   121	
   122	## Support
   123	
   124	Report bugs and request features through [GitHub Issues](https://github.com/petercr/ultimate-html-penpot-ext/issues). Include the browser, Penpot version, input type (pasted HTML or URL), and any diagnostics shown by the plugin. Do not attach confidential source HTML to a public issue.
